          "@type": "Question",
          "@id": "https://nacho09021973.github.io/bombelli/#what-is-project-en",
          "url": "https://nacho09021973.github.io/bombelli/#what-is-project-en",
          "inLanguage": "en",
          "name": "What is the Bombelli and Meyer 1987 causal-set revival?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "inLanguage": "en",
            "text": "It is a Python 3.12 port and empirical audit of the Pascal simulated-annealing program in Appendix A.2 of Luca Bombelli’s 1987 thesis, written in collaboration with David Meyer. José Ignacio Martín Gandul created the 2026 revival, with public code, tests and data.",
            "citation": [
              {
                "@type": "CreativeWork",
                "name": "Project README",
                "url": "https://github.com/nacho09021973/bombelli/blob/main/README.md"
              },
              {
                "@type": "CreativeWork",
                "name": "Full project paper",
                "url": "https://github.com/nacho09021973/bombelli/blob/main/paper/bombelli_revival_2026.md"
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "@id": "https://nacho09021973.github.io/bombelli/#what-is-causal-set-en",
          "url": "https://nacho09021973.github.io/bombelli/#what-is-causal-set-en",
          "inLanguage": "en",
          "name": "What is a causal set, and is it the same as a QCA?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "inLanguage": "en",
            "text": "A causal set is a locally finite partially ordered set: its order represents causal precedence. It is not a quantum cellular automaton, which specifies local quantum dynamics on a lattice. This project searches for Minkowski coordinates compatible with a small causal set; it does not simulate a QCA.",
            "citation": [
              {
                "@type": "CreativeWork",
                "name": "Original causal-set paper (1987)",
                "url": "https://doi.org/10.1103/PhysRevLett.59.521"
              },
              {
                "@type": "CreativeWork",
                "name": "QCA/QW student atlas",
                "url": "https://nacho09021973.github.io/atlas-qca-students/"
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "@id": "https://nacho09021973.github.io/bombelli/#python-code-en",
          "url": "https://nacho09021973.github.io/bombelli/#python-code-en",
          "inLanguage": "en",
          "name": "Where can I find Python code for causal-set embedding?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "inLanguage": "en",
            "text": "The public nacho09021973/bombelli repository contains cones.py, benchmark inputs, tests and an MIT license. Its simulated annealer searches for a low-energy coordinate embedding in Minkowski spacetime. The README explains how to download and run it.",
            "citation": [
              {
                "@type": "CreativeWork",
                "name": "Source code",
                "url": "https://github.com/nacho09021973/bombelli/blob/main/cones.py"
              },
              {
                "@type": "CreativeWork",
                "name": "Run instructions",
                "url": "https://github.com/nacho09021973/bombelli/blob/main/README.md"
              },
              {
                "@type": "CreativeWork",
                "name": "MIT license",
                "url": "https://github.com/nacho09021973/bombelli/blob/main/LICENSE"
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "@id": "https://nacho09021973.github.io/bombelli/#annealing-results-en",
          "url": "https://nacho09021973.github.io/bombelli/#annealing-results-en",
          "inLanguage": "en",
          "name": "Does changing the cooling schedule improve the causal-set embedding?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "inLanguage": "en",
            "text": "On the published 12-element benchmark, T0=100 and alpha=0.9 yield 0/100 runs with final energy below 1e-6; T0=180 and alpha=0.8 yield 95/100. The tuned schedule was selected by an empirical grid scan. This result concerns that benchmark and optimizer, not arbitrary causal sets or a general proof of embeddability.",
            "citation": [
              {
                "@type": "CreativeWork",
                "name": "Published comparison CSV",
                "url": "https://github.com/nacho09021973/bombelli/blob/main/data/schedule_comparison.csv"
              },
              {
                "@type": "CreativeWork",
                "name": "Experiment and success threshold",
                "url": "https://github.com/nacho09021973/bombelli/blob/main/experiments.py"
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "@id": "https://nacho09021973.github.io/bombelli/#reproduce-results-en",
          "url": "https://nacho09021973.github.io/bombelli/#reproduce-results-en",
          "inLanguage": "en",
          "name": "Where are the data, and how can I reproduce the 100-seed comparison?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "inLanguage": "en",
            "text": "The committed comparison is data/schedule_comparison.csv. From the repository root, run python experiments.py schedule to regenerate it with the fixed schedules, 100 seeds starting at 1959, and SCHEDULE_DIM=3. This is the reproduction command; a single demonstration run does not reproduce the ensemble.",
            "citation": [
              {
                "@type": "CreativeWork",
                "name": "Comparison data",
                "url": "https://github.com/nacho09021973/bombelli/blob/main/data/schedule_comparison.csv"
              },
